{
  "subtype": "what_if",
  "outcome_variable": "resolution_time",
  "per_column": [
    {
      "name": "incident_category",
      "causal_role": "confounder",
      "named_in_query": false,
      "rationale": "The type of incident (email, database, vpn) is not intervenable — you cannot decide to make an incident be a database issue — but it plausibly affects both how incidents are routed/handled and the resolution time, so it acts as a common-cause confounder."
    },
    {
      "name": "failure_mode",
      "causal_role": "confounder",
      "named_in_query": false,
      "rationale": "The nature of the failure (connection failure, crash, access denied) is an immutable characteristic of the incident that jointly influences handling decisions and resolution time; a confounder, not a settable lever."
    },
    {
      "name": "affected_system_tier",
      "causal_role": "confounder",
      "named_in_query": false,
      "rationale": "Which tier is affected is a structural property of the incident, not something an actor sets to reduce resolution time; it can influence both assignment and duration."
    },
    {
      "name": "recurrence_signal",
      "causal_role": "confounder",
      "named_in_query": false,
      "rationale": "Whether the incident recurs is an observed property that may correlate with both prioritization/handling and resolution time; not intervenable, so treated as a confounder."
    },
    {
      "name": "is_assigned",
      "causal_role": "treatment",
      "named_in_query": false,
      "rationale": "Whether an incident is assigned is an intervenable operational lever — management can decide to assign incidents — and plausibly affects resolution time; a valid what-if treatment."
    },
    {
      "name": "resolved_by_assignee",
      "causal_role": "mediator",
      "named_in_query": false,
      "rationale": "Whether the assignee ultimately resolved it is downstream of assignment and reflects the resolution process itself; it lies on the causal path rather than being a common cause, so it is a mediator/collider-like variable, not a confounder."
    },
    {
      "name": "is_open_incident",
      "causal_role": "outcome",
      "named_in_query": false,
      "rationale": "Open/closed status is essentially a reflection of whether resolution has occurred; it is definitionally entangled with the outcome, not an independent treatment or confounder."
    },
    {
      "name": "assigned_agent",
      "causal_role": "treatment",
      "named_in_query": false,
      "rationale": "Which agent is assigned is a genuinely intervenable assignment decision that can be set/changed and could affect resolution time; a valid intervenable treatment."
    },
    {
      "name": "caller_id_group",
      "causal_role": "confounder",
      "named_in_query": false,
      "rationale": "The caller is not something the service org sets, but caller characteristics may relate to both routing and resolution time; a weak common-cause confounder."
    }
  ],
  "treatment_present": true,
  "treatment_columns": ["is_assigned", "assigned_agent"],
  "treatment_intervenable": true,
  "confounders": [
    {"name": "incident_category", "named_in_query": false, "plausibility": 0.7},
    {"name": "failure_mode", "named_in_query": false, "plausibility": 0.6},
    {"name": "affected_system_tier", "named_in_query": false, "plausibility": 0.6},
    {"name": "recurrence_signal", "named_in_query": false, "plausibility": 0.4},
    {"name": "caller_id_group", "named_in_query": false, "plausibility": 0.3}
  ],
  "confounder_quality": 0.55,
  "rationale": "The added columns do set up a genuine interventional question: 'is_assigned' and 'assigned_agent' are settable operational levers that an actor can decide, satisfying the intervenability requirement (a). Several columns (incident_category, failure_mode, affected_system_tier, recurrence_signal, caller_id_group) function as common-cause confounders that are NOT named in the query, demonstrating the capability under test for requirement (b). However, quality is only moderate: some columns are mediators (resolved_by_assignee) or outcome reflections (is_open_incident) rather than confounders, and the assignment treatments themselves lack adjustment for agent workload/skill which would be the key confounders of the assigned_agent effect. The confounder set is plausible but not tightly tailored to blocking backdoor paths for the identified treatments."
}

